    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1379">

                <pb facs="1481" n="1467" xml:id="pg1467"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.3431/12–1844</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Paraguay</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Beaulac</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 361</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Asunción</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-12-18">December 18,
                        1944</date>.<lb/>[Received December 29.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to the
                    Department’s confidential instruction No. 49, November 27, 1944,<anchor n="28"
                        xml:id="d1379-f1"/> forwarding a revised Schedule I and authorizing the
                    Embassy to transmit it to the Paraguayan Government, or to withhold transmission
                    pending the arrival of a revised Schedule II. In the same instruction the
                    question of tariff classification of Paraguayan <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >caña</hi> was again reviewed.</p>
                <p>Since the Embassy’s restricted despatch No. 314 of December 2, 1944,<anchor
                        n="28" xml:id="d1379-f2"/> informal discussions have taken place with the
                    Foreign Office, and at its request the Embassy has suggested officially to the
                    Foreign Office that negotiations be renewed. A copy of the Embassy’s Note in
                    that regard is attached to this despatch.<anchor n="28" xml:id="d1379-f3"/>
                    While the holidays will undoubtedly cause some delay, the Foreign Office has
                    promised to expedite the negotiations and has expressed the hope that they may
                    continue without interruption until the agreement is successfully concluded.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Willard</hi> L. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Beaulac</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
